---
import {
  AffiliateButton,
  ComparisonTable,
  FAQSection,
  RelatedArticles,
} from "../../components/article";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "How to Reduce Background Noise on Video Calls (2025)";
const description =
  "Practical tips and software solutions to eliminate background noise during remote meetings. From free apps to hardware upgrades for professional call quality.";
const pubDate = new Date("2024-11-24");
const updatedDate = new Date("2024-11-24");
const authorSlug = "marcus-chen";
const category = "Audio";
const categorySlug = "gear";
const readingTime = 8;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1587560699334-cc4ff634909a-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "Person on video call with noise-canceling setup";

const headings = [
  { text: "Free Stuff That Helps", slug: "free-stuff" },
  { text: "Software Options", slug: "software-options" },
  { text: "Hardware If You Need It", slug: "hardware" },
  { text: "Room Acoustics", slug: "room-acoustics" },
  { text: "What To Do (In Order)", slug: "what-to-do" },
  { text: "Questions", slug: "faq" },
];

const softwareComparisonColumns = [
  { key: "software", label: "Software", width: "140px" },
  { key: "price", label: "Price" },
  { key: "bestFor", label: "Best For" },
  { key: "platform", label: "Platform" },
];

const softwareComparisonRows = [
  {
    software: "<strong>Krisp</strong>",
    price: "$8/mo",
    bestFor: "All-around best",
    platform: "Windows/Mac",
  },
  {
    software: "<strong>NVIDIA Broadcast</strong>",
    price: "Free",
    bestFor: "NVIDIA GPU owners",
    platform: "Windows",
  },
  {
    software: "<strong>RTX Voice</strong>",
    price: "Free",
    bestFor: "Older NVIDIA cards",
    platform: "Windows",
  },
  {
    software: "<strong>Zoom Built-in</strong>",
    price: "Free",
    bestFor: "Zoom users",
    platform: "All",
  },
  {
    software: "<strong>Teams Built-in</strong>",
    price: "Free",
    bestFor: "Teams users",
    platform: "All",
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Everyone hears me typing?",
    answer:
      "Condenser mics pick up everything. That's what they do. Switch to dynamic or use Krisp. Or just mute.",
  },
  {
    question: "Noise cancellation makes me sound robotic?",
    answer:
      "Happens if settings are too aggressive. Krisp does this well. Built-in platform stuff varies. Record yourself and check.",
  },
  {
    question: "Echo problems?",
    answer: "Speakers instead of headphones. That's it 90% of the time. Wear headphones.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I actually need all this?",
    answer:
      "Client calls where you're closing deals? Probably fix it. Internal meetings where everyone's used to your barking dog? Maybe less urgent. Your call.",
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Microphones for Remote Meetings",
    excerpt: "Upgrade your mic for clearer audio that rejects background noise.",
    href: "/gear/best-microphones-remote-meetings",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1590602847861-f357a9332bbc-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Professional USB microphone for remote work",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Noise-Canceling Headphones",
    excerpt: "Block distractions and prevent echo with quality headphones.",
    href: "/gear/best-noise-canceling-headphones",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1505740420928-5e560c06d30e-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Noise-canceling headphones on desk",
  },
  {
    title: "USB vs XLR Microphones",
    excerpt: "Understanding microphone types and which reduces noise best.",
    href: "/gear/usb-vs-xlr-microphones",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1598653222000-6b7b7a552625-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Professional microphone setup comparing USB and XLR",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    updatedDate={updatedDate}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    category={category}
    categorySlug={categorySlug}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Lost a client because my dog barked. Yep. Kickoff call, she wouldn't shut up, guy says "I don't think this is going
    to work out" and hangs up. Three grand. Gone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Background noise matters. Learned that one the hard way.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here's the thing though—most people buy expensive mics thinking that solves everything. I did. Dropped $200 on a
    Shure MV7. Guess what? My neighbor's leaf blower doesn't care how fancy your microphone is.
  </p>

  <p>
    What actually works is boring. Free stuff mostly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="free-stuff">Free Stuff That Helps</h2>

  <p>
    Move your face closer to the mic. Like 4-6 inches. Not kidding. Your voice gets louder compared to everything else
    in the room. Beats expensive gear. Tested it myself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wear headphones. Literally any headphones. Those $15 earbuds from the gas station? Fine. Without them, your mic
    picks up audio from your speakers and creates this echo that drives everyone crazy. Sat through calls where
    someone's voice bounced back three times. Painful.
  </p>

  <p>
    Close your doors. Yeah, duh. But I forget constantly. My place faces a busy street—one closed door cuts noise in
    half. Works even if you live alone because sound bounces off open doorways.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fans and AC? Turn them off before important calls. My ceiling fan through the Shure sounds like a helicopter.
    Figured that out embarrassingly late.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your place is just loud? Mute when you're not talking. Zoom: Alt+A. Teams: Ctrl+Shift+M. Learn it. Use it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="software-options">Software Options</h2>

  <p>
    The noise removal apps have gotten weirdly good. Like actually good.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable columns={softwareComparisonColumns} rows={softwareComparisonRows}/>

  <h3>Krisp</h3>
  <p>
    Krisp is my pick. Eight bucks a month. Filters out my dog, construction next door, typing sounds—pretty much
    everything. Free tier gives 60 minutes weekly. Might be enough depending on your call load.
  </p>
  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/krisp" text="Try Krisp Free"/>
  </div>

  <h3>NVIDIA Broadcast</h3>
  <p>
    Got an NVIDIA RTX card? 2060 or newer? NVIDIA Broadcast does this free. Borrowed a friend's gaming laptop to test
    it. Solid. Also blurs your background if your bedroom's a mess.
  </p>

  <h3>Built-in Platform Options</h3>
  <p>
    But most people don't know: your video app already has this built in.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zoom</strong> hides it in Settings → Audio → "Suppress background noise." Set to High.</li>
    <li><strong>Teams:</strong> Settings → Devices → Noise Suppression. High.</li>
    <li><strong>Meet:</strong> three dots → Settings → Audio → toggle noise cancellation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tested all three. Zoom's version was... okay. Not great. Krisp still wins.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hardware">Hardware If You Need It</h2>

  <p>
    Sometimes software won't cut it.
  </p>

  <h3>Dynamic Mics</h3>
  <p>
    Dynamic mics work different than condensers. Less sensitive. Pick up what's right in front of them and ignore the
    rest. The <strong>Shure MV7</strong> is dynamic. So is the <strong>Audio-Technica ATR2100x</strong>. Both good.
  </p>
  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/shure-mv7" text="Check Shure MV7 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3>Headphones</h3>
  <p>
    Noise-canceling headphones with decent mics work too. <strong>Sony XM5s</strong>. <strong>Bose 700s</strong>. Pricey
    but you'll use them for music and flights, so.
  </p>
  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/sony-wh-1000xm5" text="Check Sony XM5 Price"/>
  </div>

  <h3>Boom Arm + Shock Mount</h3>
  <p>
    Boom arm made a real difference for me. Mic goes near my face, off my desk. No more picking up my nervous
    finger-tapping or keyboard clacking. Add a shock mount ($20-30) and vibrations disappear.
  </p>

  <h3>Audio Interface</h3>
  <p>
    For the nerds: Focusrite Scarlett Solo ($110-ish) plus an XLR mic beats any USB mic's noise floor. Overkill for most
    people though.
  </p>

  <h2 id="room-acoustics">Room Acoustics</h2>

  <p>
    Moved my desk from a big empty room to a smaller bedroom. Massive improvement.
  </p>

  <p>
    Smaller rooms with carpet and furniture just sound better. Basic physics.
  </p>

  <p>
    Thick curtains help. Rugs too. Bookshelves work better than bare walls. My wife's mountain of pillows that I
    complained about for years? Great for acoustics apparently.
  </p>

  <p>
    Foam panels are like $20-40. Put them behind your mic. Helps some. Picking the right room helps more honestly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-to-do">What To Do (In Order)</h2>

  <p>
    Start simple.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>First:</strong> Close doors. Wear headphones. Get close to mic. Free. Two minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Second:</strong> Turn on your app's noise suppression. Also free.</li>
    <li><strong>Third:</strong> Still bad? Try Krisp's free tier or NVIDIA Broadcast.</li>
    <li><strong>Fourth:</strong> Need better? Hardware time—dynamic mic, boom arm, better headphones.</li>
    <li><strong>Fifth:</strong> Really serious? Acoustic treatment.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Most people stop at step two or three. Rest is for podcast-level quality or truly bad spaces.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqItems}/>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
